WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:06.200
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.425
We are now ready to learn about

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:13.320
the key algorithmic technique called
dynamic programming, and

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.435
to learn about the dynamic programming we

00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:20.640
will look at the very simple change
property.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.530
You walk to the supermarket, you pay

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:29.511
for Compeau and Pevzner book,
and you have 40 cents in change.

00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:31.783
And now cashier is making a decision,

00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.330
how to give you this change?

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:37.070
The cashier can give you a quarter, a
dime,

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.820
an extra 5 cents, I think it will be three
coins.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.750
But cashier also has a choice of giving
you 40 pence.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.510
The change problem is to find the minimum
number

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:52.420
of coins to change a given amount of
money.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.320
And the first thing that come to mind is
actually the approach that cashiers

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.010
all over the world use.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.360
They simply look at the amount of money
they

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.150
have to change, and they find the maximum
denomination,

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.010
maximum coin that
doesn't exceed the money.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.320
They give you this coin, subtract this

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.580
coin from the amount of money, and
iterate.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.990
Do we think this approach would produce an
optimal solution?

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.730
In fact, it will produce an optimal
solution

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:29.188
in United States, but not in Tanzania.
Tanzania has beautiful coin,

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:35.362
shown here, and they actually, in addition to
quarters, they have a 20 cents coin.

00:01:35.362 --> 00:01:38.519
So, while great algorithm, in Tanzania,

00:01:38.519 --> 00:01:41.291
will result in three coins as a change,

00:01:41.291 --> 00:01:48.110
because it starts from 25 as largest coin, smaller
than 40, then it's ten, then it's five.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:52.827
The right thing to change money in
Tanzania is simply to give you

00:01:52.827 --> 00:01:57.445
two 20 cents coin.
So what algorithm should we design to

00:01:57.445 --> 00:02:03.790
change money in Tanzania optimally?
Let's design a recursive change algorithm.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.024
And let's imagine that we are in a strange
country where

00:02:07.024 --> 00:02:12.340
there are only three coins: 6, 5 and 1 cents.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.445
How would we change

00:02:13.445 --> 00:02:16.570
9 cents using these coins.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:21.540
Well to change 9 cents, to find optimal
amount of money (coins)

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.460
optimal means number of coins to change
9 cents, we can find

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:33.170
minimum number of coins to change 8 cents
and to add 1 penny.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.714
Or minimum number of coins to change 4 cents
and to add 5 cents coin

00:02:39.714 --> 00:02:44.595
one more coin, or to find the minimum
number of coins to change

00:02:44.595 --> 00:02:49.648
3 cents and add more coin, right.
And therefore the minimum number of coins for

00:02:49.648 --> 00:02:56.472
9 cents is the minimum of the minimum number of
coins for

00:02:56.472 --> 00:02:59.508
8, 4 or 3 cents plus one (coin)

00:02:59.508 --> 00:03:04.000
Makes sense?
Okay.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.860
but, to find the minimum number of coins

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.410
for 3, 4 and 8 cents, we once again need

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.430
to find the minimum number of coins for other
values

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.169
of money, and we will be doing this
recursively.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:24.360
So, the general formula for whatever
number of coins we may have for denomination

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.450
will be the minimum number of coins for
the amount of money equal to the minimum

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.430
of the following d expressions.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:38.040
Where d is the number of coins in the
currency.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:48.398
It is the minimum number of coins for money
minus coin one, minus coin two...

00:03:48.398 --> 00:03:52.630
And finally minimum number of coins for
money

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.570
minus coin d.
Plus one for all these values.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.570
And there is a simple recursive

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:03.610
change algorithm that
implements this approach.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:11.190
So every time we need to change the amount
of coins equal to money, we have

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.530
d recursive calls.
And compute the minimum number of change

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:21.234
for money minus coin i.
Do you think this is a good algorithm?

00:04:21.234 --> 00:04:26.820
This algorithm is definitely correct,

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:32.710
but it will take quite enormous time to solve
the problem.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.500
Let's see how it works.
Let's start from 76 cents

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.724
and see how our algorithm works.
To compute the change for

00:04:40.724 --> 00:04:45.819
76 cents, we need to compute

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:49.972
first the change for 70 cents, 71 cents,

00:04:49.972 --> 00:04:54.410
and 75 cents

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:04.410
And we continue this way recursively, and
we see that to compute (the answer for) 76 cents,

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.789
we actually need to compute the optimal amount
of change for 69 cents twice.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.010
But if we go further, we see that

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.210
we actually need to compute it more than
twice.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:19.460
Now we see that we need to compute it
five (more) times.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.360
In reality, we actually need to compute

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.648
the optimal coin combination for 69 cents
six times.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:34.940
Okay, but how many times do we need to
compute optimal combination for 30 cents?

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:40.960
It turns out that we need to compute it
trillions of times,

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.220
which means that our algorithm is correct,
but it will (practically) never end.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.710
Because it takes so much time.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:52.630
We need to come up with a different
approach.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.710
Instead of changing money greedily,

00:05:54.710 --> 00:06:00.020
let's try something different.
Wouldn't it be wonderful to compute

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:06.040
all the values minimum number of coins for
money minus coin i.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:09.390
That all values that we need to compute

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.648
minimum number of coins for the amount of
money.

00:06:11.648 --> 00:06:16.790
If we pre-computed all those values, then
computed minimum number of

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:20.300
coins for a given amount of money would be
a simple

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.870
exercise, and a very fast exercise, right?

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.440
And to implement it the only thing
 that we need,

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.830
instead of the time-consuming calls
lead to recursive change for

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:37.560
money minus coin i,
we simply look up the values

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.050
of the minimum number of coins for
money minus coin.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.040
So how our algorithm changes?

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:51.760
We'll, instead of moving from larger
values to smaller values to fill

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.240
this table, we will now move from smaller
values to larger values.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.740
For example, what is the minimum number of
coins to change

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:00.780
zero cents?

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.670
Of course it is zero.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.090
What is the minimum number of coin to
change one cent?

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.110
Of course it is one.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:07.940
Two?
It is two.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:07.970
Three? It is three.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.050
And four, it is four.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.030
And now we come to changing
five coins.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.690
And they can be changed in two different
ways.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:20.990
They can be changed either by taking
minimum number

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:24.590
of coins to change zero, and adding one
coin.

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:28.640
Or minimum number of coins to change four,
and adding one coin.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.730
Which of these numbers is smaller?

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:37.980
This is a simple check, and we choose to
change five coin by adding

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.900
a 5 cents coin to the existing combination.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:45.320
Now, we are facing a problem
of changing 6 cents.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.960
In this case there are three choices.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:53.950
We can either use optimal change for 5 cents
or 1 cent or 0 cents.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:59.790
And depending which of them is the
smallest, will decide how to change 6 cents.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:03.380
So in this case It turns out that the best
strategy

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:07.550
is simply to add 6 cent coin to the
combination.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:12.950
We continue further and further until we
arrive to changing nine coins.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.840
Of course, it is a much faster algorithm
and

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:23.090
we can implement it differently without
any recursive calls.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:28.540
Simply, at every stage to compute the amount
of coins (for a concrete money), we need to

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.010
look at d previously computed values

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:36.820
where d is the number of
denominations.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:38.960
That's all.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.900
That's the idea developed by Richard
Bellman called dynamic programming,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:49.060
which is arguably the most popular
algorithmic technique today.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:53.720
Amazingly, 60 years ago, when Richard
Bellman was developing

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:57.090
this technique, it was absolutely unclear

00:08:57.090 --> 00:09:01.610
that this technique has any practical
applications.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:06.940
In fact, Richard Bellman was working on a
project for Air Force, and

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:09.060
it turned out, some students sometimes ask you

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.140
where is the programming
in the dynamic programming?

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.370
It turns out there is no
programming in dynamic programming

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:20.812
because at that time, his
approach seemed completely impractical.

00:09:20.812 --> 00:09:23.580
And he worked for the Air Force, so he
wanted to

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:28.520
hide that he was really doing mathematics
from the secretary of defense.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.930
He was a prominent mathematician by this
time.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:35.100
This is a quote from Richard Bellman.
What name could I choose?

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.100
I was interested in planning, but

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.130
planning is not a good word for various
reasons.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:44.350
I decided, therefore, to use the word
programming.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.380
And I wanted to get across the

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.720
idea that this was dynamic.
It was something not even

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:57.800
a congressman could object to, so I use it
as an umbrella for my activities.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:04.080
Amazingly enough, the most popular
algorithmic technique today was invented

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.910
as an umbrella for a mathematical activity

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:11.230
while working on the Air Force project.

